<!-- These resources are around just to allow their values to be customized
     for different hardware and product builds.  Do not translate. -->
<resources>
    <!-- Control whether the always on display mode is available. This should only be enabled on
         devices where the display has be tuned to be power efficient in DOZE and/or DOZE_SUSPEND
         states.

         FALSE, and it must stay false, because this panel cannot doze (GitHub issue #13).
         AOSP's own condition for setting this true is DOZE/DOZE_SUSPEND support, and this
         device has none of the three things that make it real:
           1. BOARD_USES_DOZEMODE is not set anywhere in device/samsung/{a30s,
              exynos7885-common}, so hardware/samsung_slsi-linaro/config/hwc.mk:66 never
              sets the exynos_hwc soong bool USES_DOZEMODE, so
              ExynosDisplayFbInterface::isDozeModeAvailable() returns false
              (graphics/base/libhwc2.1/display/ExynosDisplayFbInterface.h:79), so
              exynos_getDozeSupport() reports no support and the composer never advertises
              DisplayCapability.DOZE (hwc3/ComposerClient.cpp:195).
           2. # CONFIG_EXYNOS_DOZE is not set - in arch/arm64/configs/
              full/exynos7885-a30s_defconfig:2881 AND in the built
              out/target/product/a30s/obj/KERNEL_OBJ/.config - so decon_doze.c and
              dsim_doze.c are not even compiled into the kernel.
           3. Not one panel driver in drivers/video/fbdev/exynos/dpu_7885/panels/ implements
              the AOD entry path (grep enter_doze_mode|doze_init -> 0 files). dsim_doze.c is
              generic DSI plumbing; the panel sequence that would drive the pixels in doze
              does not exist for this panel. See changelog_v.08.md 21.3.

         WHAT SETTING IT TRUE ACTUALLY DID (v0.8.0, measured by a tester on SM-A307FN 4 GB,
         issue #13): SystemUI's doze_display_state_supported is false (frameworks/base/
         packages/SystemUI/res/values/config.xml:200, and the doze.display.supported sysprop
         is unset), so DozeScreenStatePreventingAdapter.wrapIfNeeded installs a wrapper whose
         setDozeScreenState() rewrites STATE_DOZE to STATE_ON and STATE_DOZE_SUSPEND to
         STATE_ON_SUSPEND (doze/DozeScreenStatePreventingAdapter.java:40-45). AOD therefore
         never meant "low power panel": it meant the panel stayed powered at 0 brightness
         with the display suspend blocker held, and the SoC could not suspend at all with the
         screen apparently off. Measured: 2428 of 2436 mAh charged-as-screen-on over 11 h 38 m,
         "screen off" time 5.9 s, /sys/class/backlight/panel/brightness=1 while dozing.
         doze_always_on=0 makes dozeScreenState go to STATE_OFF and the device suspends.

         WHY FALSE RATHER THAN "DEFAULT AOD TO OFF": AOD is not merely off by default here,
         it is unreachable, and that has to be true for users who already turned it on too.
         AmbientDisplayConfiguration.alwaysOnEnabledSetting() ANDs the stored
         Settings.Secure.DOZE_ALWAYS_ON with alwaysOnAvailable(), which reads this resource
         (core/java/android/hardware/display/AmbientDisplayConfiguration.java:303-308, 339-341,
         374-376). So one OTA flips every existing install to AOD-off with no wipe and no
         user action, whereas config_dozeAlwaysOnEnabled=false (the setting DEFAULT, used only
         while the key is unset) would leave anyone who had toggled it on, and every Settings
         entry point that hides itself when AOD is unavailable (qs/tiles/AODTile.java:106,
         SystemServer.java:2920 AutoAODService) would hide the switch they would need.

         The one bypass that resource does NOT cover is the "always-on while charging" key
         (Settings.Secure.DOZE_ON_CHARGE), whose getter in AmbientDisplayConfiguration ignored
         availability entirely; that is closed in the same fix, see
         core/java/android/hardware/display/AmbientDisplayConfiguration.java
         alwaysOnChargingEnabled().

         To get real AOD on this board someday, all three numbered items above have to be
         implemented (panel AOD sequence first - nothing downstream works without it), then
         BOARD_USES_DOZEMODE + CONFIG_EXYNOS_DOZE, then doze_display_state_supported and a
         non-zero config_screenBrightnessDoze. Until then do not re-enable this resource: a
         DOZE request the composer rejects is not a no-op, it is a lit panel. -->
    <bool name="config_dozeAlwaysOnDisplayAvailable">false</bool>

    <!-- If true, the display will be shifted around in ambient mode. -->
    <bool name="config_enableBurnInProtection">true</bool>

    <!-- Whether device supports double tap to wake -->
    <bool name="config_supportDoubleTapWake">true</bool>

   <!-- The bounding path of the cutout region of the main built-in display.
         Must either be empty if there is no cutout region, or a string that is parsable by
         {@link android.util.PathParser}.
         The path is assumed to be specified in display coordinates with pixel units and in
         the display's native orientation, with the origin of the coordinate system at the
         center top of the display.
         To facilitate writing device-independent emulation overlays, the marker `@dp` can be
         appended after the path string to interpret coordinates in dp instead of px units.
         Note that a physical cutout should be configured in pixels for the best results.
         Example for a 10px x 10px square top-center cutout:
                <string ...>M -5,0 L -5,10 L 5,10 L 5,0 Z</string>
         Example for a 10dp x 10dp square top-center cutout:
                <string ...>M -5,0 L -5,10 L 5,10 L 5,0 Z @dp</string>
         @see https://www.w3.org/TR/SVG/paths.html#PathData
         -->
   <string translatable="false" name="config_mainBuiltInDisplayCutout">M 0,0 H -67.42857142857143 V 30.85714285714286 H 67.42857142857143 V 0 H 0 Z @dp</string>
   <string translatable="false" name="config_mainBuiltInDisplayCutoutRectApproximation">@*android:string/config_mainBuiltInDisplayCutout</string>
</resources>
